  "method": "K=20 LGBM residual on chemprop_aux anchor with hessian-aware init_score warm-start: init_score = median(residual_fold_train) (L1-optimal scalar warm-start passed via fit(init_score=...)). Standard nb27xx hyperparams (max_depth=4, num_leaves=15, n_est=300, lr=0.03). 5-fold scaffold CV mean-bag over 5 kf_seeds.",
  "paradigm": "hessian_init_warm_start_LGBM",
  "novelty": "Warm-start / base-score regularizer distinct from prior nb27xx tree-structure (nb2774 symmetric), loss-shape (nb2762 Cauchy, nb2754 focal, nb2722 Huber), feature-subsampling (nb2152 colsample), and linear-leaf (nb2750) attempts. LightGBM defaults to init_score=0 so the first ~lr^-1=33 boosting rounds spend structural budget recovering the constant offset; by supplying init_score = median(train_residual) (the L1-optimal constant), the first tree's leaves can immediately allocate splits to feature signal, lowering early-round variance. Critically, init_score is computed from fold-TRAIN ONLY (never validation), and the same constant is added back to validation/test predict outputs so the OOF protocol stays leak-free. xgboost calls this base_score, sklearn HGB calls it init, LightGBM exposes it as init_score -- the canonical gradient-boosting warm-start lever.",
